Release 0.2.0.

Changelog: 17-10-2026 14:24:42

Release 0.1.1.

Changelog: 17-10-2026 14:05:45

Fixed. The app's folder in the suite is apps/wdriver (was apps/usb-wifi) and its package is usb_wifidriver (was
bc250_usb_wifi): the suite's naming convention (owner, 17-10-2026), a short folder name and a package name without
the BC-250 prefix; both names are the owner's. With the folder go its key in the portal, its chapter of the wiki
(apps/wdriver/, which the Manual link and the service unit's Documentation line open), its installer test and its
release tags: wdriver-v<x.y.z> from the next release on, which the app's update check follows. The installed app
keeps its name, its launcher bc250-usb-wifi, its folder under ~/.local/share (an install replaces the whole app
folder there, so no old package folder stays behind), its settings, and the driver's files and service: the service
runs its own copy of driver.py by path, not the package. That copy now differs from the app's (the wiki link), so
after this update the window asks for Update once, as after any change to driver.py; until then the installed
service keeps working as before.

Changelog: 17-10-2026 11:26:44

Fixed. The window test compared the install command's --app-version with the literal 0.1.0, so it failed as soon
as VERSION was bumped; it compares with the package's __version__ now.

Changelog: 17-10-2026 11:06:03

Release 0.1.1.

Changelog: 17-10-2026 10:24:01

Fixed. The wiki chapter has a section Compatibility: the driver is for the AIC8800 family (the USB IDs its match
tables claim, under both of AICSemi's vendor IDs and Tenda's and UGREEN's), the app installs the AIC8800D80
firmware only, the window recognizes the a69c storage, loader and Wi-Fi IDs and the eject rule covers vendor a69c,
the modules follow the running kernel, and what was tested (the UGREEN AX900 on the BC-250).

Changelog: 17-10-2026 05:35:56

Release 0.1.0.

Changelog: 17-10-2026 05:05:00

Added. BC-250 USB Wi-Fi, a new app: the driver for AIC8800D80 USB Wi-Fi sticks (the UGREEN AX900 and others),
which Bazzite's kernel does not have. The app shows the stick as the board sees it (storage mode, waiting for its
firmware, Wi-Fi mode), the chip's answer from the kernel log and the driver branch it takes (chip_mcu_id 1 needs
the legacy-mcu1 branch of shenmintao/aic8800d80 with the SDK 3 firmware; the SDK 5 firmware of main is larger than
that chip takes, so its upload breaks off at 0x170400), builds the driver as your own user from a pinned commit
against the running kernel's build tree (gcc, make and kernel-devel are in Bazzite's image; git fetches the one
commit), and installs it through the password dialog: the modules and the firmware under /var/lib/bc250-usb-wifi
(Bazzite's /usr is read-only), a udev rule that ejects the stick's virtual disk so it leaves storage mode, and a
system service (driver.py, standard library only, in /etc) that loads the modules at every boot with the firmware
folder as a parameter, ejects a stick in storage mode, and after a kernel update builds the driver again at the next
boot from the kept sources, with a desktop notification either way. The window shows what the service did last.
Removing the driver unloads it and takes every file away; uninstalling the app removes the driver too (sudo). The
stick's firmware does WPA2, not WPA3, which the window says. Proven on the owner's board (17-10-2026): 5 GHz at
200 Mbit/s, and a Console Link stream over it that equals the cable's. Two safeguards for the build (owner,
17-10-2026): the sources are fetched from a mirror under the suite's account first and from the original repository
when the mirror does not answer, so a vanished upstream stops no fresh install; and "Newest sources (untested)"
fetches the branch's head instead of the pin, for the day a new kernel breaks the pinned build before a release
moves the pin, with the installed driver marked as newest in the window.
